.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_CTX_CONFIG 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_CTX_config ,
.Nm SSL_config
.Nd configure SSL_CTX or SSL structure
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_CTX_config
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "const char *name"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_config
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fa "const char *name"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The functions
.Fn SSL_CTX_config
and
.Fn SSL_config
configure an
.Vt SSL_CTX
or
.Vt SSL
structure using the configuration
.Fa name .
.Pp
By calling
.Fn SSL_CTX_config
or
.Fn SSL_config
an application can perform many complex tasks based on the contents of
the configuration file: greatly simplifying application configuration
code.
A degree of future proofing can also be achieved: an application can
support configuration features in newer versions of OpenSSL
automatically.
.Pp
A configuration file must have been previously loaded, for example using
.Xr CONF_modules_load_file 3 .
See
.Xr config 5
for details of the configuration file syntax.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_CTX_config
and
.Fn SSL_config
return 1 for success or 0 if an error occurred.
.Sh EXAMPLES
If the file "config.cnf" contains the following:
.Bd -literal
 testapp = test_sect

 [test_sect]
 # list of configuration modules

 ssl_conf = ssl_sect

 [ssl_sect]
 server = server_section

 [server_section]
 RSA.Certificate = server-rsa.pem
 ECDSA.Certificate = server-ecdsa.pem
 Ciphers = ALL:!RC4
.Ed
.Pp
An application could call:
.Bd -literal
 if (CONF_modules_load_file("config.cnf", "testapp", 0) <= 0) {
     fprintf(stderr, "Error processing config file\en");
     goto err;
 }

 ctx = SSL_CTX_new(TLS_server_method());

 if (SSL_CTX_config(ctx, "server") == 0) {
     fprintf(stderr, "Error configuring server.\en");
     goto err;
 }
.Ed
.Pp
In this example two certificates and the cipher list are configured
without the need for any additional application code.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7 ,
.Xr config 5 ,
.Xr SSL_CONF_cmd 3 ,
.Xr CONF_modules_load_file 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn SSL_CTX_config
and
.Fn SSL_config
functions were added in OpenSSL 1.1.0.
